## What shipped

`tests/gasp_doc_version.rs` → `tests/doc_version_claims.rs`, widened from one hardcoded file to a scan of src/**/*.rs + CLAUDE.md. (The rename's delete half had already been swept into an auto-commit, 6befc0e8.)

• Scan, not hardcode — pure `markers_in(source) -> Vec<(line, version)>` over every file, all I/O at the call site. Failure collects *all* mismatches and prints each as file:line, claimed, resolved, and the sentence "re-read the paragraph above this marker — it may have gone stale".
• Exactly-once retired, replaced with something stronger. Once the marker is a convention, "exactly one in the repo" forbids the second consumer. What survives: every marker agrees with `Cargo.lock` plus at least one marker exists — a scanner that finds nothing and passes is the vacuous-green shape.
• Second live marker on the MemoryEventStore claim in task_result's doc. Same genre (what an external dep cannot do), load-bearing (it's the stated reason four ported arms have no behavioural test), and it rots silently if upstream re-exports the store. No substitution needed — the claim was where the task said it would be.
• CLAUDE.md: convention paragraph beside the other three deterministic gates, plus the two stale spots in the gasp.rs bullet (tests/gasp_doc_version.rs, the exactly-once rule).

## Both failure paths were run, not assumed

Edited marker → 0.16.3: fired with the full remedy text. Broke the marker string repo-wide: the at-least-one assertion fired with "a scanner with nothing to scan passes for the wrong reason." Restored both. A gate whose failure path was never executed is a gate whose message is a guess.

## Limits stated in the test's own doc comment

A marker proves someone looked at the current version, never that the prose is correct — presence is mechanically checkable, truth is not. No blanket scan for "impossible"/"unreachable" wording; no markers on claims about my own code.

Green: build, test (5074 + 4 new), clippy --all-targets -D warnings, fmt --check, and clippy --features gasp (the module I edited only compiles under that flag).

One detail I enjoyed: CLAUDE.md now describes the convention without becoming an instance of it, because the colon is what makes a marker — and there's a unit test pinning exactly that.
